author_id=40374652)  [1](#full-view-affiliation-1 \"Department of Pediatrics, Seoul National University College of Medicine, Seoul National University Children's Hospital, Seoul, South Korea.\") , [Ki Joong Kim](/?term=Kim+KJ&cauthor_id=40374652)  [1](#full-view-affiliation-1 \"Department of Pediatrics, Seoul National University College of Medicine, Seoul National University Children's Hospital, Seoul, South Korea.\") , [Seungbok Lee](/?term=Lee+S&cauthor_id=40374652)  [3](#full-view-affiliation-3 \"Department of Pediatrics, Seoul National University College of Medicine, Seoul National University Children's Hospital, Seoul, South Korea. for3guy@naver.com.\")   [4](#full-view-affiliation-4 \"Department of Genomic Medicine, Seoul National University Hospital, Seoul, South Korea. for3guy@naver.com.\") , [Jong-Hee Chae](/?term=Chae+JH&cauthor_id=40374652)  [1](#full-view-affiliation-1 \"Department of Pediatrics, Seoul National University College of Medicine, Seoul National University Children's Hospital, Seoul, South Korea.\")   [2](#full-view-affiliation-2 \"Department of Genomic Medicine, Seoul National University Hospital, Seoul, South Korea.\")\n\nAffiliations Expand\n\n### Affiliations\n\n-   1 Department of Pediatrics, Seoul National University College of Medicine, Seoul National University Children's Hospital, Seoul, South Korea.\n-   2 Department of Genomic Medicine, Seoul National University Hospital, Seoul, South Korea.\n-   3 Department of Pediatrics, Seoul National University College of Medicine, Seoul National University Children's Hospital, Seoul, South Korea. for3guy@naver.com.\n-   4 Department of Genomic Medicine, Seoul National University Hospital, Seoul, South Korea. for3guy@naver.com.\n\n-   PMID: **40374652**\n-   PMCID: [PMC12081769](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12081769/)\n-   DOI: [10.1038/s41525-025-00499-z](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41525-025-00499-z)\n\nItem in Clipboard\n\n# Exploring gene-phenotype relationships in GRIN-related neurodevelopmental disorders\n\nJong Ho Cha et al. NPJ Genom Med. 2025.\n\nShow details\n\nDisplay options\n\nDisplay options\n\nFormat Abstract PubMed PMID\n\nNPJ Genom Med\n\nActions\n\n-   [Search in PubMed](/?term=%22NPJ+Genom+Med%22%5Bjour%5D&sort=date&sort_order=desc)\n-   [Search in NLM Catalog](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/nlmcatalog?term=%22NPJ+Genom+Med%22%5BTitle+Abbreviation%5D)\n-   [Add to Search](#)\n\n. 2025 May 15;10(1):40.\n\ndoi: 10.1038/s41525-025-00499-z.\n\n### Authors\n\n[Jong Ho Cha](/?term=Cha+JH&cauthor_id=40374652)  [1](#short-view-affiliation-1 \"Department of Pediatrics, Seoul National University College of Medicine, Seoul National University Children's Hospital, Seoul, South Korea.\") , [Jee Min Kim](/?term=Kim+JM&cauthor_id=40374652)  [1](#short-view-affiliation-1 \"Department of Pediatrics, Seoul National University College of Medicine, Seoul National University Children's Hospital, Seoul, South Korea.\") , [Hee-Jeong Yun](/?term=Yun+HJ&cauthor_id=40374652)  [1](#short-view-affiliation-1 \"Department of Pediatrics, Seoul National University College of Medicine, Seoul National University Children's Hospital, Seoul, South Korea.\") , [Hyungjin Chin](/?term=Chin+H&cauthor_id=40374652)  [1](#short-view-affiliation-1 \"Department of Pediatrics, Seoul National University College of Medicine, Seoul National University Children's Hospital, Seoul, South Korea.\") , [Hye Jin Kim](/?term=Kim+HJ&cauthor_id=40374652)  [1](#short-view-affiliation-1 \"Department of Pediatrics, Seoul National University College of Medicine, Seoul National University Children's Hospital, Seoul, South Korea.\") , [Woojoong Kim](/?term=Kim+W&cauthor_id=40374652)  [1](#short-view-affiliation-1 \"Department of Pediatrics, Seoul National University College of Medicine, Seoul National University Children's Hospital, Seoul, South Korea.\") , [Soo Yeon Kim](/?term=Kim+SY&cauthor_id=40374652)  [1](#short-view-affiliation-1 \"Department of Pediatrics, Seoul National University College of Medicine, Seoul National University Children's Hospital, Seoul, South Korea.\")   [2](#short-view-affiliation-2 \"Department of Genomic Medicine, Seoul National University Hospital, Seoul, South Korea.\") , [Byung Chan Lim](/?term=Lim+BC&cauthor_id=40374652)  [1](#short-view-affiliation-1 \"Department of Pediatrics, Seoul National University College of Medicine, Seoul National University Children's Hospital, Seoul, South Korea.\") , [Ki Joong Kim](/?term=Kim+KJ&cauthor_id=40374652)  [1](#short-view-affiliation-1 \"Department of Pediatrics, Seoul National University College of Medicine, Seoul National University Children's Hospital, Seoul, South Korea.\") , [Seungbok Lee](/?term=Lee+S&cauthor_id=40374652)  [3](#short-view-affiliation-3 \"Department of Pediatrics, Seoul National University College of Medicine, Seoul National University Children's Hospital, Seoul, South Korea. for3guy@naver.com.\")   [4](#short-view-affiliation-4 \"Department of Genomic Medicine, Seoul National University Hospital, Seoul, South Korea. for3guy@naver.com.\") , [Jong-Hee Chae](/?term=Chae+JH&cauthor_id=40374652)  [1](#short-view-affiliation-1 \"Department of Pediatrics, Seoul National University College of Medicine, Seoul National University Children's Hospital, Seoul, South Korea.\")   [2](#short-view-affiliation-2 \"Department of Genomic Medicine, Seoul National University Hospital, Seoul, South Korea.\")\n\n### Affiliations\n\n-   1 Department of Pediatrics, Seoul National University College of Medicine, Seoul National University Children's Hospital, Seoul, South Korea.\n-   2 Department of Genomic Medicine, Seoul National University Hospital, Seoul, South Korea.\n-   3 Department of Pediatrics, Seoul National University College of Medicine, Seoul National University Children's Hospital, Seoul, South Korea. for3guy@naver.com.\n-   4 Department of Genomic Medicine, Seoul National University Hospital, Seoul, South Korea. for3guy@naver.com.\n\n-   PMID: **40374652**\n-   PMCID: [PMC12081769](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12081769/)\n-   DOI: [10.1038/s41525-025-00499-z](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41525-025-00499-z)\n\nItem in Clipboard\n\nFull text links Cite\n\nDisplay options\n\nDisplay options\n\nFormat AbstractPubMedPMID\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe GRIN family is implicated in neurological disorders, such as global developmental delay (GDD) and epilepsy. We reviewed 31 patients with GRIN-related neurodevelopmental disorders at Seoul National University Hospital; all exhibited profound GDD, with 58.1% unable to walk independently and 74.2% unable to speak meaningful words. In a pooled analysis with the GRIN portal data ( https://grin-portal.broadinstitute.org/ ), patients with missense or in-frame variants had significantly higher rates of profound GDD (74.3% vs. 30.4%, p < 0.001) and movement disorders (69.0% vs. 41.4%, p < 0.01) than those with protein-truncating variants. Furthermore, missense or in-frame variants in the M3 and M4 helices of the transmembrane domain were significantly associated with profound GDD (M3 helix: adjusted odds ratio \\[aOR\\] 8.48; 95% confidence interval \\[CI\\] 2.79-25.76; M4 helix: aOR 3.14; 95% CI 1.39-7.09) compared to those in other domains. Our findings highlight the importance of detailed variant characterization to inform personalized treatment strategies.\n\n© 2025. The Author(s).\n\n[PubMed Disclaimer](/disclaimer/)\n\n## Conflict of interest statement\n\nCompeting interests: The authors declare no competing interests.\n\n## Figures\n\n [![Fig. 1](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/99d8/12081769/cc51039fbd5a/41525_2025_499_Fig1_HTML.gif)](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/99d8/12081769/a2b91e4364ca/41525_2025_499_Fig1_HTML.jpg) \n\n**\n\nFig. 1. Representative cases of *GRIN* \\-related…\n\n**\n\n**\n\nFig. 1. Representative cases of *GRIN* \\-related neurodevelopmental disorders.\n\n**\n\n**a** Clinical information of SNUH 2, who…\n\n**Fig. 1. Representative cases of *GRIN*\\-related neurodevelopmental disorders.**\n\n**a** Clinical information of SNUH 2, who presented with a chief complaint of profound GDD at four months of age. The patient was found to have a de novo *GRIN1* missense variant located in the M4 helix of TMD (c.2443G>C, p.Gly815Arg). An initial brain MRI acquired at four months of age showed delayed myelination, while follow-up imaging at two years old showed diffuse brain atrophy. **b** Clinical information of SNUH 29 and SNUH 30, monozygotic twins presenting with a chief complaint of profound GDD. Quartet whole-exome sequencing analysis identified a de novo *GRIN2B* missense variant (c.2287G>A, p.Gly763Ser) in both twins. Brain MRI acquired at 12 months old revealed nodular T2 hyperintensities in the brainstem (SNUH 29, SNUH 30) and cerebellum (SNUH 30). TMD transmembrane domain, GDD global developmental delay, MRI magnetic resonance imaging.\n\n [![Fig. 2](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/99d8/12081769/454c7754cbb1/41525_2025_499_Fig2_HTML.gif)](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/99d8/12081769/fc3a267f4bb2/41525_2025_499_Fig2_HTML.jpg) \n\n**\n\nFig. 2. Prevalence of neurological phenotypes among…\n\n**\n\n**\n\nFig. 2. Prevalence of neurological phenotypes among pooled variants (including data from the GRIN Portal…\n\n**\n\n**Fig. 2. Prevalence of neurological phenotypes among pooled variants (including data from the GRIN Portal and the SNUH cohort), stratified by gene type and variant type.**\n\nPrevalence of neurological phenotypes stratified by **a** gene type and **b** variant type. \\**p* < 0.05. ASD autism spectrum disorder, GDD global developmental delay, MD movement disorder, CVI cortical visual impairment, MCD malformation of cortical development, PTV protein-truncating variant.\n\n [![Fig. 3](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/99d8/12081769/1c87788eb7b4/41525_2025_499_Fig3_HTML.gif)](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/99d8/12081769/2bd89a1c66c5/41525_2025_499_Fig3_HTML.jpg) \n\n**\n\nFig. 3. Information on pathogenic *GRIN* variants…\n\n**\n\n**\n\nFig. 3. Information on pathogenic *GRIN* variants identified in the ClinVar database and the SNUH…\n\n**\n\n**Fig. 3. Information on pathogenic *GRIN* variants identified in the ClinVar database and the SNUH cohort.**\n\n**a** A pie chart illustrating the proportion of *GRIN* variants categorized by gene type. **b** The number and proportion of pathogenic variants (PTVs vs. missense or in-frame variants in other domains vs. missense or in-frame variants in the M3–M4 helices). **c** Genomic locations of pathogenic variants identified in *GRIN1, GRIN2A, GRIN2B*, and *GRIN2D*. The blue density plots represent the domain-specific distribution of missense or in-frame variants from the GRIN Portal and the SNUH cohort. Lollipops in the upper row indicate variant information from the SNUH cohort. Lollipops in the bottom row indicate pathogenic/likely pathogenic missense or in-frame variants reported in ClinVar. PTV protein-truncating variant.\n\n [![Fig. 4](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/99d8/12081769/32a0eb2ef138/41525_2025_499_Fig4_HTML.gif)](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/99d8/12081769/b044c25c841c/41525_2025_499_Fig4_HTML.jpg) \n\n**\n\nFig. 4. Prevalence and risk of neurological…\n\n**\n\n**\n\nFig. 4. Prevalence and risk of neurological phenotypes among pooled variants (including data from the…\n\n**\n\n**Fig. 4. Prevalence and risk of neurological phenotypes among pooled variants (including data from the GRIN Portal and the SNUH cohort), stratified by variant domains.**\n\n**a** Comparisons among M3–M4 helices, other domains, and PTVs. **b** Prevalence and risk of neurological phenotypes across missense or in-frame variant domains (other domains, M3 helix, and M4 helix of each *GRIN* gene) presented as odds ratios with 95% confidence intervals, adjusted for *GRIN* gene subtypes. a*p* < 0.05 for comparisons between missense or in-frame variants in M3–M4 helices and those in other domains. b*p* < 0.05 for comparisons between in-frame variants in the M3–M4 helices and PTVs. c*p* < 0.05 for comparisons between missense or in-frame variants in other domains and PTVs. *\\*p* < 0.05. GDD global developmental delay, ASD autism spectrum disorder, MD movement disorder, CVI cortical visual impairment, MCD malformation of cortical development.\n\n[See this image and copyright information in PMC](#)\n\n## References\n\n1.  1.  Langer, K., Müller-Längle, A., Wempe, J. & Laube, B. Analysis of M4 transmembrane segments in NMDA receptor function: a negative allosteric modulatory site at the GluN1 M4 is determining the efficiency of neurosteroid modulation. *Front. Pharmacol.***12**, 769046 (2021). - [DOI](https://doi.org/10.3389/fphar.2021.769046) - [PMC](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8517087/) - [PubMed](/34658899/)\n2.  1.  Paoletti, P., Bellone, C. & Zhou, Q. NMDA receptor subunit diversity: impact on receptor properties, synaptic plasticity and disease. *Nat. Rev. Neurosci.***14**, 383–400 (2013). - [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1038/nrn3504) - [PubMed](/23686171/)\n3.  1.  Platzer, K. et al. GRIN2B encephalopathy: novel findings on phenotype, variant clustering, functional consequences and treatment aspects. *J. Med. Genet.***54**, 460–470 (2017). - [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1136/jmedgenet-2016-104509) - [PMC](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5656050/) - [PubMed](/28377535/)\n4.  1.  Strehlow, V. et al. GRIN2A-related disorders: genotype and functional consequence predict phenotype. *Brain***142**, 80–92 (2019). - [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1093/brain/awy304) - [PMC](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6308310/) - [PubMed](/30544257/)\n5.  1.  XiangWei, W., Jiang, Y. & Yuan, H. De novo mutations and rare variants occurring in NMDA receptors. *Curr. Opin. Physiol.***2**, 27–35 (2018). - [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cophys.2017.12.013) - [PMC](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5945193/) - [PubMed](/29756080/)\n\nShow all 28 references\n\n## Grants